<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Religious pluralism</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Religious pluralism is the coexistence of several religions within a single society or state. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate</a> was notable for accommodating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and traditional beliefs, which eased commercial relations with diverse partners. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhimmi">dhimmi</a> framework allowed Christians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrians</a> to practise their faiths in exchange for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a> tax.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/religious-pluralism.jpg" alt="Religious pluralism" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maarten · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kazan_church.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Religious pluralism is an attitude or policy regarding the diversity of religious belief systems that coexist in a society. It can mean recognising and tolerating that diversity, promoting freedom of religion, and treating the state as neutral on religion. It can also mean a form of inclusivism, in which one's own religion is not the sole source of truth, or the view that religions with mutually exclusive claims are equally valid. Perennialism holds that the exclusive claims of different religions are variations of universal truths. The term is sometimes used as a synonym for ecumenism, or for the harmonious coexistence of adherents of different religions.</p><p>Chris Beneke distinguishes pluralism from toleration. By the 1730s most American colonies had granted religious toleration, which relieved minorities of some penalties and burdens but did not make them equal, since they could still be barred from civil offices and university posts. Between the 1760s and the 1780s the colonial governments replaced it with what is usually called religious liberty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The German philosophers Ludwig Feuerbach and Ernst Troeltsch concluded that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> were the earliest proponents of religious pluralism. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a> and Daoism have also long favoured it. In ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> religion was part of daily life, with household shrines, neighbourhood shrines and a calendar of as many as 135 days of festivals and games in the imperial era. According to Chad Meister, pluralist trends in Western thought since the 18th century brought mainstream <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> closer to the Asian traditions of philosophical pluralism and tolerance.</p><p>In early Islamic history, Muhammad declared religious freedom for Muslims, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and pagans in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-medina">Constitution of Medina</a>. The Islamic caliphate later guaranteed religious freedom to non-Muslim communities that accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhimmi">dhimmi</a> status, with adult males paying the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a> tax in place of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakat">zakat</a>. Dhimmis did not have the same political rights as Muslims, but they enjoyed equality under the laws of property, contract and obligation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Religious traditions answer pluralism in different ways. The Catholic and Eastern Orthodox churches each claim to be the one true church, and most Christians consider the maximal form, that all religions are equally true, logically impossible, though the philosopher John Hick argues for a version of pluralism. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>'s principle of anekantavada holds that reality is perceived differently from different points of view, so that no single view is the complete truth. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-gurus">Sikh gurus</a> taught many paths to one God.</p><p>The Bahá'í Faith teaches that religion has been revealed progressively through founders such as Abraham, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>, Buddha, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> and Muhammad. Voltaire's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candide">Candide</a> of 1759 is among the places where the argument from inconsistent revelations appears: that revelations contradict one another, so that one cannot choose among them.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Religious pluralism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Khazar society was marked by <b>religious pluralism</b>: Judaism, Christianity, Islam and traditional Tengrist beliefs were all practised, which eased commercial dealings with a varied range of partners.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>This model of regulated <b>religious pluralism</b>, despite its inequalities, allowed the relatively rapid stabilisation of conquered territories.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
